{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nClosing `cb2b-7446-748c-47bc` through MCP `transition_ticket` failed with \"5 unmet criteria\" for work already merged as `e21f3240cd0`. The findings cited exact lines that were true of a different tree: the MCP server process's checkout, whose HEAD `2e28d817a7e` does not contain `e21f3240cd0`. The agent worked in a linked worktree that the server never reads. The same ticket passed and signed with `ref=\"e21f3240cd0\"` (A/B in the first comment).\n\nMechanism (confirmed in the code and by a reproduction in the evidence comment): `transition_close.close_ticket` pins the default target to the server process's HEAD (`_pin_completion_ref`, `src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py:128`). The close precheck `close_precheck._check_work_landed` finds no referencing commit on that HEAD, refreshes remote refs, and accepts the commit from the upstream refs (`include_upstream=True`, `close_precheck.py:196-203`). The verifier then reads the pinned HEAD anyway. So the two halves of the gate disagree about the tree, and the FAIL message (`_verification_fail_message`, `close_precheck.py:441`) names no SHA. The PASS path records `verified_at_sha`; the FAIL path does not show it.\n\nDesign intent this keeps: the default tree stays the invoking process's HEAD (ticket `seaworthy-revengeful-blobfish` classed this \"correct by design\"), and `ref` remains the documented way to target another commit (`docs/plan-review-gate.md` §\"Which commit the completion gate verifies — `--ref`\").\n\n## Approach\n\nMake the wrong-tree case self-diagnosing and refuse it before any LLM cost, without changing which tree is read by default.\n\n1. `_check_work_landed` returns the landed commit list it already computes (`landed`), or `[]` when no `file_impact` applies.\n2. New `close_scope.ensure_tree_contains_landed(ticket_id, landed, code_root, tree_sha, tree_source)` runs `git merge-base --is-ancestor <commit> <tree_sha>` for each landed commit. Commits absent from the clone (exit status other than 0 or 1, e.g. an attached SHA from another clone) are left out of the comparison. If at least 1 landed commit is present and none is contained in the tree, it raises `CommandError` (exit 1) naming the tree's full SHA, its source, the landed commits, and the remedy `ref=<landed commit>` (CLI `--ref`). It lives in `close_scope.py` because that module already owns the precheck's evidence scope, and `close_precheck.py` is 741 of the 800-line cap.\n3. `_completion_precheck` gains an optional `ref_source` keyword. It resolves the tree (`ref`, or `HEAD` of `code_root` when `ref` is None), labels it `HEAD of <code_root>` or `explicit ref '<ref>'`, calls the new check after `_check_work_landed` and before `_verify_with_duration_metrics`, and passes the label into `_verification_fail_message`.\n4. `_verification_fail_message` gains an optional `tree` keyword and prints `Verified tree: <40-char sha> (<source>)`, using the result's `verified_at_sha` when present.\n5. `close_ticket` records `explicit ref '<ref>'` before pinning when the caller passed `ref` and forwards it. It goes through a small helper so `close_ticket` gains no branch (complexity baseline 17).\n6. Docs: state that over MCP the default tree is the server process's checkout, describe the refusal, and name `ref` as the remedy.\n\nThe existing stale-clone tests `test_file_impact_close_fetches_before_rejecting_stale_clone` and `test_file_impact_close_succeeds_when_local_and_remote_branch_names_differ` (`tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_completion_gate.py:372,397`) assert that a close succeeds when the referencing commit is only on the remote. Under this fix that is exactly the mismatch, so they change to assert the refusal names the fetched commit (not \"no commit references it\"), and that the same close with `ref=<that commit>` succeeds. That keeps the stale-clone ticket's contract (`autophobic-serge-booby` AC1: \"fetches before resolving, or it detects staleness and says so\").\n\n## Scope\n\n- src/rebar/_commands/close_precheck.py — return `landed`; call the tree check; tree label in the FAIL message\n- src/rebar/_commands/close_scope.py — `ensure_tree_contains_landed`\n- src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py — forward the explicit-ref label\n- docs/plan-review-gate.md — MCP default-tree passage\n- tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_completion_tree_mismatch.py — new regression tests\n- tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_completion_gate.py — update the 2 stale-clone tests\n- tests/unit/test_close_duration_instrumentation.py — `_check_work_landed` now returns the landed list\n\n## Testing\n\n- Failure path (AC2, AC4): server HEAD `D`, landed commit `C` only on the remote, `C` not an ancestor of `D`; the close refuses, the stubbed verifier is never called, and stderr names `D` and `C`.\n- Remedy: the same close with `ref=C` passes and signs at `C`.\n- Genuine FAIL (AC1): HEAD contains the commit; stderr names HEAD's full SHA with `(HEAD of `; with an abbreviated explicit ref, it names the full SHA with `(explicit ref '<abbrev>')`.\n- Regression: the existing completion-gate, ref-target, and duration suites stay green.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 A completion-verification FAIL names the tree it read (full 40-char SHA and its source: `HEAD of <path>` or `explicit ref '<ref>'`) in the failure message. Verified by `pytest tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_completion_tree_mismatch.py -k fail_names`.\n- [ ] AC2 When the verification tree contains none of the landed commits the precheck accepted, the close refuses with a mismatch message naming both and the `ref` remedy, and emits no per-criterion findings. Verified by `pytest tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_completion_tree_mismatch.py -k refuses_tree`.\n- [ ] AC3 `git grep -nE 'verif(y|ication)' -- docs/plan-review-gate.md` surfaces a passage stating that over MCP the default tree is the server process's checkout, not the caller's worktree, and naming `ref` as the way to target the landed commit.\n- [ ] AC4 A regression test reproduces the shape (worktree commit `C`, server checkout `D`, `C` not an ancestor of `D`) and asserts the verifier is not run against `D`: the same `refuses_tree` test, which fails on the unfixed tree.\n- [ ] AC5 `pytest tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_completion_gate.py tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_close_ref_target_80af.py tests/unit/test_close_duration_instrumentation.py` passes, `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass, and the Gerrit change receives `Verified +1` (CI runs the full `make test` selection)."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793520297418470001,"uuid":"f95a5775-cd21-4198-abb8-376b951c0122"}